---
/**
 * website/src/components/CapsCosts.astro — the caps-and-costs guide (website plan, Task
 * W1.5; reworked by the owner's brief of 2026-10-19).
 *
 * The page answers three questions in this order, and the order is the point:
 *
 *   1. **What a job costs.** The page leads with a small table — one row per kind of run,
 *      with the sample size, the median, the middle half and the figure nine runs in ten
 *      stayed under — rendered from `website/data/typical-costs.yaml`, the owner's own
 *      measurement of this project's development runs. It is a hand-written fact file, and
 *      the page writes no figure of its own: every number below is the file's, and
 *      `website/test/caps-and-costs.mjs` fails on a figure the file does not carry or a
 *      figure the file carries that the page does not render (Global Constraint 6, D96,
 *      D97).
 *   2. **How to set the caps.** The commands are the detail and they sit below the table,
 *      behind one `<details>`: a reader who came for what a job costs meets the figures
 *      first, and the whole of `fugaro budget` is one click away. Every command is a
 *      `Usage:` line and a one-line description read out of `website/content/cli-reference.md`
 *      (W0.4, generated from the command tree), so the guide cannot print a flag the command
 *      does not ship and a renamed flag reaches this page the day the reference is
 *      regenerated (finding 26, Global Constraint 6). The page writes no command of its own
 *      — `website/test/caps-and-costs.mjs` asserts that nothing here carries `fugaro budget`
 *      outside an element the reference filled.
 *   3. **What the owner's own aggregation publishes.** `website/content/costs.json` is what
 *      the owner's run of W0.3's command writes, and the page publishes the per-recipe
 *      ranges it holds — each with the window and the sample size behind it — or, while the
 *      file is absent, the empty state, which is the honest state on day one (D96, D97). No
 *      figure is written here, and none is rounded: a rounded figure is one the JSON does
 *      not carry, and the same test fails on any number the file does not contain.
 *
 * What this file deliberately does not do: it does not restate the reference's long
 * descriptions (they carry the amounts' own bounds, which are the command's business and
 * not a page's), it does not print a total either file does not carry, and it does not
 * describe a group the aggregation suppressed. A thin group is absent from the JSON and the
 * page renders what is there; a file that carries one anyway stops the build in W1.3's
 * pipeline rather than reaching a reader (D96's answer fixes the minimum at 20 runs).
 *
 * **What the rework of 2026-10-19 took out, and what it added.** The page used to open with
 * the caps and the whole command list — a reader asking what a run costs met the command
 * tree first — and the measured figures were last. The owner's brief turns that around: the
 * figures lead, the commands are the detail behind one `<details>`, and the aggregation's
 * ranges stay last. The per-command scope sentences went with it, because D188's 250-word
 * ceiling is a ceiling and the reference's own one-line descriptions already say what each
 * command does; the lead line the parent command carried went the same way. What the page
 * gained is the table, its provenance note, and the two rules that hold it: the file's
 * figures are the page's figures, and the page's figures are the file's.
 *
 * **A phone reads the figures as labelled rows** (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19: the
 * figures, which are the right-hand part of the page on a desktop, "render terrible on
 * mobile"). Both tables say the same columns in the same order and print the same figures;
 * what changes below the site's 48 rem breakpoint is the layout — each row is a block, each
 * cell on its own line, each cell labelled with its column's own header text through
 * `data-label`, and the figures set at the body's size rather than the table's smaller one.
 * Nothing here invents a figure, a label or a claim: the label is the header's text, and
 * `website/test/caps-figures.mjs` holds every cell's label to its column's header and the
 * phone's layout to the rules that make it (a table read as a table at 360 px is a sideways
 * scroll or six crushed columns, and both tables would have been that).
 *
 * **The cap the note prints is the installation's, not the product's.** `fugaro budget
 * set --per-run N` is what sets a per-run cap: the flag's default is 0, which sets none, and an
 * enforcing project with no per-run cap on any of the three scopes refuses every lease
 * (`internal/cli/budget.go`, and "Set caps" in `docs/gcp-setup.md`). So the figure below is
 * Fugaro's own installation's cap, the one these runs ran under, and a refresh of
 * `website/data/typical-costs.yaml` confirms it with the installation rather than assuming it.
 * The note says whose cap it is; it does not read as something `fugaro` ships by default.
 *
 * **The word ceiling, measured in both states of the aggregation** (recorded here because it is
 * a conflict between two owner rulings rather than a defect of this page, and the number is what
 * the next task needs). D188's ceiling is 250 words for a page, and this page measures **249**
 * while `website/content/costs.json` is absent — the state this checkout builds, and so the
 * state `website/test/budget.mjs` reads. With a file published the same page measures **451**:
 * the empty state is 22 words, and the aggregation's own window, its counted-runs lines, its
 * table and the aggregation's "not a promise" disclaimer are about 230 more. D96 and D97 ask
 * for the window, the sample size, the counts and that disclaimer to be published, and D188
 * counts every word a reader sees, so publishing the file (W2.3) is the task that has to choose
 * which rule gives way — the five of them cannot all hold at once. Both numbers are measured
 * with the gate's own reader over a build, not argued.
 */
import { loadSite } from '../content.ts'
import { copyControlHTML } from '../lib/copy-control.mjs'

const site = loadSite()

// The generated reference, as bytes. It is a required document of the content pipeline, so
// its header and its body have already been checked before this line runs (W1.3).
const reference = site.documents['cli-reference.md'].body

interface ReferenceCommand {
  /** The command path the reference's heading names, e.g. `fugaro budget set`. */
  path: string
  /** The reference's one-line description: cobra's own `Short`, as generated. */
  short: string
  /** The reference's `Usage:` line: cobra's own `Use` string, as generated. */
  usage: string
}

/**
 * The reference's budget sections: its heading, its one-line description and its usage
 * line. The generated document's shape is fixed by `internal/clidoc` (`## \`fugaro x\``,
 * then the short description, then the long one, then `Usage: \`...\``), and this reader
 * takes exactly those three facts and refuses to guess at anything else.
 */
function readReference(body: string): Map<string, ReferenceCommand> {
  const commands = new Map<string, ReferenceCommand>()
  const lines = body.split('\n')
  let current: ReferenceCommand | null = null
  let sawShort = false

  for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
    const heading = /^## `(.+)`$/.exec(lines[i])
    if (heading) {
      if (current !== null) commands.set(current.path, current)
      current = { path: heading[1], short: '', usage: '' }
      sawShort = false
      continue
    }
    if (current === null) continue
    if (!sawShort) {
      // The first non-empty line under the heading is the command's one-line description.
      if (lines[i].trim() !== '') {
        current.short = lines[i].trim()
        sawShort = true
      }
      continue
    }
    if (current.usage === '' && lines[i].startsWith('Usage: `')) {
      current.usage = /^Usage: `(.+)`$/.exec(lines[i])?.[1] ?? ''
    }
  }
  if (current !== null) commands.set(current.path, current)
  return commands
}

const commands = readReference(reference)

/**
 * The commands the guide prints, in the order a reader meets them: the caps and the mode,
 * what is set now, the estimate before a batch runs, the price table behind it, and both
 * halves of the kill switch.
 *
 * The same array is the coverage the guide owes — every subcommand of `fugaro budget` the
 * reference carries, checked just below, so a subcommand added to the command tree cannot
 * be left off the page while the page says it prints the whole of the interface. What each
 * command does is the reference's own line, rendered beside it, and not a claim of this
 * file's.
 */
const GUIDE = [
  'fugaro budget set',
  'fugaro budget show',
  'fugaro budget preview',
  'fugaro budget prices',
  'fugaro budget kill',
  'fugaro budget resume',
]

/**
 * The guide's claim is coverage, so coverage is what is checked: the reference carries the
 * whole `fugaro budget` command tree, and a subcommand it has and this guide does not is a
 * sentence on the page that is false — the F50 class of defect, a public claim nothing
 * backs. The build stops rather than dropping a subcommand silently, which is why the page
 * may say it prints the whole of the interface. A subcommand added to the command is
 * carried by the regenerated reference and lands here, or the rebuild stops.
 */
const uncovered = [...commands.keys()].filter(
  (path) => path.startsWith('fugaro budget ') && !GUIDE.includes(path),
)
if (uncovered.length > 0) {
  throw new Error(
    `the CLI reference carries ${uncovered.join(', ')}, which the caps guide does not cover: this page prints the whole of the command's interface, so an omitted subcommand makes its own sentence false. Add it to GUIDE in website/src/components/CapsCosts.astro, or stop the page claiming coverage (W1.5).`,
  )
}

for (const path of ['fugaro budget', ...GUIDE]) {
  const command = commands.get(path)
  if (command === undefined || command.short === '' || command.usage === '') {
    throw new Error(
      `website/content/cli-reference.md carries no usable section for \`${path}\`: the caps guide prints the CLI reference's own usage lines and one-line descriptions, so a command the reference does not carry is a command this page cannot print. Regenerate the reference with \`go run ./internal/clidoc\` (W0.4, W1.5).`,
    )
  }
}

/** The figures the owner measured on this project's own runs. */
const typical = site.typicalCosts

/** The figures the owner's run of the aggregation published, or null while it has not run. */
const costs = site.costs

/**
 * A figure as a reader reads it: dollars and cents, from the value the file carries. This is
 * padding and never rounding — `website/data/typical-costs.yaml` carries `1` for a dollar and
 * the page prints `$1.00` — and the loader makes that true rather than trusting it: a figure
 * that is not a whole number of cents is refused at the build (`asCents` in `src/content.ts`),
 * so there is no figure here for `toFixed` to round, and the test holds the printed figure to
 * the digits the file itself carries.
 */
function money(value: number): string {
  return `$${value.toFixed(2)}`
}

/**
 * The counts the page prints, each one a number the file carries or nothing at all.
 *
 * A count the file does not carry is **omitted rather than defaulted**: a template that
 * renders `undefined` puts a hole on the page, and a template that renders `0` for a
 * count nobody measured states something the aggregation did not. The command prints every
 * one of these keys (W0.3's `--json`), so in practice all of them render; the page is
 * written not to depend on that, because the counts are the aggregation's and the page's
 * job is to print what is there.
 */
function countOf(name: string): number | null {
  const value = costs?.totals[name]
  return typeof value === 'number' ? value : null
}

const counters: { label: string; value: number }[] = []
const addCounter = (name: string, label: (value: number) => string): void => {
  const value = countOf(name)
  if (value !== null) counters.push({ label: label(value), value })
}

addCounter('runs_below_min_runs', (value) => `${value} runs were below that threshold and are not grouped`)
addCounter('runs_without_a_figure', (value) => `${value} runs carried no finished figure`)
addCounter('runs_unknown_recipe', (value) => `${value} runs named a recipe this binary does not ship`)
addCounter('runs_not_estimated', (value) => `${value} runs carried no compute estimate`)

const windowRuns = countOf('runs')
const windowRecipes = countOf('recipes')
---

<section id="typical-costs">
  <h2>What a job costs</h2>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
            <th scope="col">Job</th>
          <th scope="col">Runs</th>
          <th scope="col">Median</th>
          <th scope="col">Middle half</th>
          <th scope="col">9 in 10 under</th>
          <th scope="col">Worst</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {typical.jobs.map((job) => (
          <tr data-job={job.id}>
            <th scope="row" data-label="Job">{job.name}</th>
            <td data-label="Runs">{job.runs}</td>
            <td data-label="Median">{money(job.medianUSD)}</td>
            <td data-label="Middle half">{job.middleHalfUSD === null ? '—' : `${money(job.middleHalfUSD.low)}–${money(job.middleHalfUSD.high)}`}</td>
            <td data-label="9 in 10 under">{money(job.nineInTenUnderUSD)}</td>
            <td data-label="Worst">{job.worstUSD === null ? '—' : money(job.worstUSD)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note">
    Fugaro's own runs, {typical.from} to {typical.to}: {typical.runs} finished, model plus
    compute, an estimate. Mostly DeepSeek through OpenRouter; a Claude model costs more.
    Not a quote: the caps below are the safety net; our per-run cap is {money(typical.perRunCapUSD)}.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="caps">
  <details>
    <summary><h2>Set the caps</h2></summary>

    <dl class="commands">
      {GUIDE.map((path) => {
        const command = commands.get(path)
        return (
          <div class="command" data-guide={path}>
            <dt>
              <div class="command-block" data-copy>
                <Fragment set:html={copyControlHTML('Copy the usage line')} />
                <code class="usage" data-command={command?.usage}>{command?.usage}</code>
              </div>
            </dt>
            <dd>
              <p>{command?.short}</p>
            </dd>
          </div>
        )
      })}
    </dl>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="costs">
  <h2>Per-recipe ranges</h2>

  {costs === null || costs.groups.length === 0 ? (
    <p class="empty">
      The owner's aggregation has published no range: not enough runs behind it. An empty
      figure is honest, an invented one is not.
    </p>
  ) : (
    <>
      <p class="window">Measured over the <code>{costs.project}</code> installation's own run records, from <code>{costs.since}</code> to <code>{costs.until}</code>{windowRuns !== null && windowRecipes !== null ? `: ${windowRuns} runs in the window, of which ${windowRecipes} recipes carried enough runs to publish a figure.` : '.'}</p>

      <p class="suppressed">A group thinner than the {costs.minRuns} runs this site publishes from is absent rather than approximated{counters.length > 0 ? ', and the rest of the window is counted rather than grouped:' : '.'}</p>
      {counters.length > 0 && (
        <ul class="counters">
          {counters.map((counter) => (
            <li>{counter.label}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>
            Duration and cost per recipe: the minimum, the median and the p90, with the sample
            size behind every figure.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Recipe</th>
              <th scope="col">Runs</th>
              <th scope="col">Duration, s — min / median / p90</th>
              <th scope="col">Cost, USD — min / median / p90</th>
              <th scope="col">Runs with no compute estimate</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {costs.groups.map((group) => (
              <tr data-recipe={group.recipe}>
                <th scope="row" data-label="Recipe">{group.recipe}</th>
                <td data-label="Runs">{group.runs}</td>
                <td data-label="Duration, s — min / median / p90">{group.durationS.min} / {group.durationS.median} / {group.durationS.p90}</td>
                <td data-label="Cost, USD — min / median / p90">{group.costUSD.min} / {group.costUSD.median} / {group.costUSD.p90}</td>
                <td data-label="Runs with no compute estimate">{group.computeNotEstimated}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="not-a-promise">{costs.notAPromise}</p>
    </>
  )}
</section>

<style>
  /* The guide's own box. Every space, radius and colour is a token from
     `src/styles/tokens.css`; nothing here names a value of its own, and nothing here is a
     colour literal (D104, D105). */
  section {
    margin-block: var(--space-xl);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .command {
    border-top: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    padding-block: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .usage {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--surface);
    padding: var(--space-3xs) var(--space-2xs);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .note,
  .suppressed,
  .window,
  caption {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
  }

  th,
  td {
    border-bottom: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    padding: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-xs);
    text-align: left;
  }

  /* **A phone reads the figures as labelled rows, not as a table** (the owner's feedback of
     2026-10-19: "these render terrible on mobile"). Six short columns of figures are about
     460 px of content and a phone's content box is 328 px (360 px less `main`'s gutter), so a
     table read as a table at 360 px is either a sideways scroll or six crushed columns. Below
     the site's one layout breakpoint each row becomes a block, the header row's names become
     the labels above the figures they name, and every cell is on a line of its own: the same
     columns, the same figures, in the order the table has them.

     The label is the cell's own `data-label`, and it is the column's header text exactly —
     the page writes no label of its own, and `website/test/caps-figures.mjs` holds every
     label to its column's header. `::before` with `attr()` is what makes one rule label
     every cell rather than one attribute per figure being read by a reader.

     The figures are set at the body size here and not the table size: `base.css` sets every
     `table` (`:where(table) { font-size: var(--font-size-sm) }`) at 15 px where the body is
     17 px, which is a table's smaller type for six columns and not a phone's one figure under
     its name. `overflow-wrap: anywhere` (also `base.css`) is what keeps a long recipe name
     inside the box. */
  @media (max-width: 47.98rem) {
    table,
    thead,
    tbody,
    tr,
    th,
    td {
      display: block;
    }

    /* The names are the labels now, and a header row drawn as a block would be an unlabelled
       list of six words above the first row's figures. */
    thead {
      display: none;
    }

    table {
      font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    }

    th,
    td {
      border-bottom: none;
      padding: 0;
    }

    /* One row is one card, and the line between two of them is what tells a reader where one
       run's figures stop. */
    tr {
      border-bottom: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
      padding-block: var(--space-sm);
    }

    /* `base.css` fills every `th` with the surface colour, which is a table's header cell and
       not a card's title. The row header is the title: the name of the job or the recipe the
       figures under it belong to. */
    th {
      background: none;
    }

    th[scope='row'] {
      font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
      margin-bottom: var(--space-3xs);
    }

    /* The label above its figure. It is the header's own text, in the muted colour and on a
       line of its own, so a reader at 360 px reads "Median" over the median rather than
       counting columns. */
    th[data-label]::before,
    td[data-label]::before {
      content: attr(data-label);
      display: block;
      color: var(--text-muted);
    }
  }

  /* Above the breakpoint the figures are a table again, and the two boxes that make a wide
     table fit stay here rather than in the base rules: six columns of figures held on their
     own lines are wider than a phone, so at a wide width the table scrolls inside
     `.table-scroll` and the page body never scrolls sideways. Below the breakpoint neither is
     in force — `nowrap` on a stacked figure is a line that runs out of the card, and a scroll
     container around blocks that fit is a box that can only hide what is in it. */
  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .table-scroll {
      overflow-x: auto;
    }

    #typical-costs td {
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
  }

  .empty {
    border-left: var(--border-width) solid var(--warn);
    padding-left: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .not-a-promise {
    border-left: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    padding-left: var(--space-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
